'''Iliamna Lake''' or '''Lake Iliamna''' ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|ɪ|l|i|ˈ|æ|m|n|ə}} {{respell|IL|ee|AM|nə}};<ref>{{cite web |title=Iliamna Lake - A Land Worth Preserving |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ARRHRZUBwA&t=104s |website=YouTube |access-date=9 August 2024}}</ref> {{langx|esu|label=[[Yup'ik language|Yup'ik]]|Nanvarpak}}; {{langx|tfn|label=[[Dena'ina language|Dena'ina Athabascan]]|Nila Vena}}) is a lake in southwest [[Alaska]], at the north end of the [[Alaska Peninsula]], between [[Kvichak Bay]] and [[Cook Inlet]], about {{convert|100|mi|km}} west of [[Seldovia, Alaska]].<ref name="GNIS-IL"/> It shares a name with the [[Iliamna River]], which flows into it, and the nearby community of [[Iliamna, Alaska]].

It is the largest lake in [[Alaska]], [[List of lakes of the United States by area|7th largest lake]] in the United States, and twenty-fourth in North America. Covering about {{cvt|2,600|km2}}, Iliamna Lake is {{convert|77|mi|km|0}} long and up to {{convert|22|mi|km|0}} wide, with a maximum depth of {{convert|988|ft|m}}. Through the [[Kvichak River]], its waters drain into [[Bristol Bay]].<ref name="DOI-NA">{{cite web|title=Profile of the People and Land of the United States |publisher=[[US Department of Interior]], [[National Atlas of the United States]] |url=http://www.nationalatlas.gov/articles/mapping/a_general.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120915153709/http://www.nationalatlas.gov/articles/mapping/a_general.html |archive-date=2012-09-15 }}</ref>
