==History==
[[File:AKMap-doton-Newhalen.PNG|thumb|Location of Iliamna in Alaska]]
===Name===
The lake is marked as 'Oz[ero] Bol[shoy] Ilyamna' (Big Ilyamna Lake) on the [[Russian Hydrographical Department]]'s Chart 1455, published in 1852. On an earlier Russian map, from 1802, the lake was named 'Oz[ero] Shelekhovo' (Lake Shelekov) after Russian explorer [[Grigory Shelekhov]]. According to G.C. Martin, of the [[United States Geological Survey]], Iliamna is said to be "the name of a mythical great [[blackfish]] supposed to inhabit this lake, which bites holes in the [[Aleutian kayak|bidarkas]] of bad natives."<ref name="GNIS-IL"/>

[[File:Eskimos with reindeer, Iliamna Lake, Alaska, July 26, 1917 (COBB 138).jpeg|thumb|upright|Men pose by the lake with their reindeer, 1917]]
The name Iliamna is derived from the Inland [[Dena'ina language|Dena'ina Athabascan]] name ''Nila Vena,'' which means "island's lake".<ref>{{cite book|title=Dena'ina E\nena: Tanaina Country|first1=James|last1=Kari|first2=Priscilla|last2=Russell Kari|year=1982|publisher=Alaska Native Language Center|page=17}}</ref>

==Economy==
<!-- Countless lakes have been used as means of transportation, fishing, power generation, etc, and are still used so today. All such information should be described here. Stylistically, this can be a good segue from history, connecting past uses of the lake to present-day uses. -->
===Williamsport-Pile Bay Road portage===
Originally constructed by the [[Alaska Road Commission]] during the mid 1930s, the Williamsport-Pile Bay Road is a utility-class road maintained by the [[Alaska Department of Transportation & Public Facilities]].  Connecting Pile Bay on the lake's northeast side with Williamsport, a tiny settlement on the Iliamna Bay of [[Cook Inlet]] (about {{Convert|100|mi}} southwest of [[Homer, Alaska|Homer]]), the road is {{Convert|15.5|mi|1}} long and one lane wide with four bridges.  The Williamsport-Pile Bay Road is maintained as a gravel utility road for the purpose of hauling boats and freight, and is not intended for general purpose use.  The road allows boats small enough to be hauled across the road's bridges an opportunity to [[portage]] from Cook Inlet to [[Bristol Bay]], saving a trip on the open ocean which involves traveling around the [[Alaska Peninsula]].  For this and other reasons, the road is also believed to significantly reduce fuel costs for the Lake Iliamna and Bristol Bay regions.

===Populated places===
The villages of [[Iliamna, Alaska|Iliamna]], [[Newhalen, Alaska|Newhalen]], [[Kokhanok, Alaska|Kokhanok]], [[Pedro Bay, Alaska|Pedro Bay]], [[Pope-Vannoy Landing, Alaska|Pope-Vannoy Landing]] and [[Igiugig, Alaska|Igiugig]] lie on the shores of Iliamna Lake.

===Flora and fauna===
Iliamna Lake is noted for its [[sport fishing]].  The three primary targets of anglers in the lake are trout, salmon, and grayling.  August through September is prime time for catching fat [[rainbow trout]], some of which exceed 28&nbsp;inches long.  The Kvichak River (the drainage of Lake Iliamna) is [[catch and release]] on trout (and all other native fish), but not on salmon. [[Sockeye salmon|Sockeye]] (red) and [[Chinook salmon|Chinook]] (king) salmon are consistently found in the lake and are open to harvest under [[Alaska Department of Fish and Game]] Regulations. It serves as a nursery for the largest red salmon run in the world. Red salmon spend half of their 5-year lifespan in fresh water. This is longer than any other species of salmon. Lake Iliamna also has one of few populations of [[freshwater seal]]s in the world.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Resident Harbor Seals (Phoca vitulina) in Iliamna Lake, Alaska: Summer Diet and Partial Consumption of Adult Sockeye Salmon (Oncorhynchus nerka) |journal=Aquatic Mammals |date=July 1, 2008 |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P3-1596598931.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121102101508/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P3-1596598931.html |archive-date=November 2, 2012 |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |url-status=usurped}}</ref>

==Monster legend==

Local residents have a number of stories about the alleged Iliamna Lake Monster, an unknown aquatic creature.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.juneauempire.com/news/pride-of-bristol-bay-catching-the-iliamna-lake-monster/|title = Pride of Bristol Bay: Catching the Iliamna Lake Monster|date = 28 October 2020}}</ref> Speculation exists that reported sightings may be of an undocumented population of [[white sturgeon]]. If true, this would be the most northerly population known to exist, just a few hundred miles from the Arctic Circle. [[Jeremy Wade]], presenter of Animal Planet's River Monsters, is among those who speculate these sightings of a reputed "monster" are of a white sturgeon. Others believe that it is a [[Pacific sleeper shark]]. Proponents of this theory point to a 2012 YouTube video that shows a smaller Pacific sleeper shark in King Cove Lagoon, [[King Cove, Alaska]].<ref>{{Cite web|url = https://www.adn.com/features/article/fresh-evidence-alaskas-iliamna-lake-monster-pacific-sleeper-shark-video/2012/06/24/|title = Fresh evidence that Alaska's Iliamna Lake monster is a Pacific sleeper shark? (+VIDEO)|date = 23 June 2012}}</ref> There were several new supposed sightings in 2017. The Anchorage Daily News once offered a prize of $100,000 for concrete proof of its existence.

